      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1793, Dandridge became the official county seat of newly created Jefferson County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dandridge%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town sits on the northern bank of Douglas Lake, an impoundment of the French Broad River, about 12 miles upstream from Douglas Dam.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dandridge%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dandridge once served as an overnight stop on a historic stage route between Knoxville and Abingdon, Virginia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://backroadplanet.com/historical-dandridge-jefferson-county-tennessee/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Jefferson County Courthouse, built in 1845, showcases Greek Revival architecture and now houses a museum of East Tennessee history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
